Yes, I think this is exactly the right direction.
Right now the app has platform/admin screens that can see all companies. A business owner should instead log in and see only their own company’s activity: products, batches, label orders, USSD/QR verification logs, suspicious checks, and maybe company profile/settings.
How to achieve it cleanly:
1. Add company ownership to users
   Add company_id to users, nullable for platform admins.
   Example roles could become:
   owner      = platform owner/admin, sees everything
   admin      = platform admin/operator, sees everything or limited admin
   company_owner = business owner, sees only their company
   company_staff = optional future role, limited company access
   Or keep owner/admin/operator, but add an account_type like platform vs company. I prefer the clearer role split.
2. Add relationships
   In User:
   public function company()
   {
       return $this->belongsTo(Company::class);
   }
   
   public function isCompanyUser(): bool
   {
       return filled($this->company_id);
   }
   In Company:
   public function users()
   {
       return $this->hasMany(User::class);
   }
3. Create company dashboard routes
   Something like:
   Route::middleware(['auth', 'company.user'])->prefix('company')->group(function () {
       Route::get('/dashboard', [CompanyDashboardController::class, 'index'])->name('company.dashboard');
       Route::get('/products', [CompanyProductController::class, 'index'])->name('company.products');
       Route::get('/batches', [CompanyBatchController::class, 'index'])->name('company.batches');
       Route::get('/label-orders', [CompanyLabelOrderController::class, 'index'])->name('company.label-orders');
       Route::get('/verifications', [CompanyVerificationController::class, 'index'])->name('company.verifications');
   });
4. Add middleware to enforce company access
   company.user should block users without company_id, suspended users, or users trying to access another company’s data.
   Every company dashboard query should be scoped like:
   $company = auth()->user()->company;
   
   $products = Product::where('company_id', $company->id)->get();
   
   $batches = Batch::whereHas('product', fn ($query) =>
       $query->where('company_id', $company->id)
   )->get();
   
   $labelOrders = LabelOrder::where('company_id', $company->id)->get();
   
   $logs = ScanLog::whereHas('qrCode.batch.product', fn ($query) =>
       $query->where('company_id', $company->id)
   )->latest()->get();
5. Redirect users after login based on role
   Platform users go to:
   /admin/dashboard
   Company users go to:
   /company/dashboard
6. 
Build a separate company layout
   The business-owner dashboard should be simpler than admin:
   Dashboard
   Products
   Batches
   Label Orders
   USSD Codes
   Verification Logs
   Suspicious Activity
   Company Profile
   Users, optional
   The homepage metrics could show:
   Total products
   Active batches
   Label orders
   Total QR scans
   Total USSD checks
   Genuine verifications
   Suspicious/invalid attempts
   Recent verification activity
   Most checked products


7. Add policies/tests
   Important tests:
   company owner can see own dashboard
   company owner cannot see another company’s products
   company owner cannot access admin dashboard
   platform admin can still access admin dashboard
   suspended company user cannot login/use dashboard
   verification logs are scoped to user company
My suggested implementation order:
1. Add company_id and company roles to users.
2. Update user create/edit admin form so admins can assign a company.
3. Add login redirect logic.
4. Add company.user middleware.
5. Build /company/dashboard first with read-only stats.
6. Then add company products, batches, label orders, USSD codes, and verification logs.
7. Add policies/tests to lock down cross-company access.
